 ],
 "literal": "As the sun-setting sky reddens, affliction having intensified / at the light-ceasing time, jasmine-blooming / evening, they say, those who are confused / when the crested rooster in the tall mansion crows / the great dawning dawn also is evening / the day also is evening, to those without a companion.",
 "translation": "Those who say evening is only when the sun sets, the sky turns red, distress intensifies, and the jasmine blooms at the fading of light, are surely confused. For those who are separated from their beloved, even the great dawn when the crested rooster crows in the tall mansion is evening; and the bright day itself is evening.",
 "modern": "சூரியன் மறையும் வானம் சிவந்து, துன்பம் மிகுந்து, பகல் வெளிச்சம் அழியும் பொழுதில் முல்லை மலரும் மாலைக் காலமே 'மாலை' என்று கூறுபவர்கள் அறியாமையில் மயங்கியவர்கள் ஆவர். ஏனெனில், தம் துணையைப் பிரிந்தவர்களுக்கு, கொண்டையையுடைய சேவல் பெரிய வீட்டில் கூவும் விடியற்காலமும் மாலைதான்; பகல் பொழுதும் மாலைதான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Mullai tiṇai (forest landscape), which is traditionally associated with the rainy season and the heroine's patient waiting for her husband's return. The heroine, suffering from the pain of separation, addresses her companion or speaks to herself about her constant grief. She challenges the common definition of evening, arguing that only those who have never known the pain of separation believe evening is restricted to sunset when jasmines bloom. For those who are lonely, every part of the day—including the early dawn marked by the rooster's crow and the bright daytime—feels like the agonizing evening. The key words are 'மாலை' (evening/grief) and 'துணையிலோர்க்கே' (to those without a companion), emphasizing how emotional state alters the perception of time.",
